/*!
    \page qtbridges-swift-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Swift
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Swift.

    \section1 Introduction

    Qt Bridge for Swift is a bridge between Swift and QML, designed to write
    application logic in Swift while using Qt Quick for the UI. Bridging
    mechanism is based on Swift and C++ interoperability.

    Qt Bridge for Swift is intended for Apple developers who want to experiment
    with Qt and/or QML without committing to a full C++ application. The
    repository includes example applications and Xcode templates that
    demonstrate the recommended project structure, how to model data and logic
    in Swift, and how to connect those models to QML views.

    Detailed documentation can be found at the
    \l {https://code.qt.io/cgit/qt/qtbridge-swift.git/about/}{git repo}.

    \section1 Supported platforms

    Currently, only \b {macOS (Apple Silicon)} is supported, with plans to
    extend support in the future.

    \section1 Installing Qt Bridge

    Qt Bridge is distributed as a Swift Package Manager package. You can add it
    to your project as a package dependency using either a local package
    reference or the repository URL.

    By default, Qt Bridge references a \b {Qt package} hosted in an
    \b {internal repository} that contains bundled, prebuilt Qt binaries. To
    build successfully, you must have a private SSH key with access to this
    repository. The SSH key must be placed in the \c {~/.ssh} directory, and the
    \c {~/.ssh/config} file must be configured accordingly.

    If you have access to the Qt internal repository, add it as a remote SwiftPM
    dependency. Otherwise, add Qt Bridge as a local package and build the local
    Qt package that Qt Bridge depends on.

    \section2 Importing Qt Bridge as a remote package

    \section3 Add via Xcode

    \list 1
        \li In Xcode, select \uicontrol {File} →
        \uicontrol {Add Package Dependencies}.
        \li Click the \uicontrol {Add Local} button and select the folder that
        contains cloned Qt Bridge repo.
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability:
        \list 1
            \li Go to the target's \uicontrol {Build Settings}.
            \li Switch the filter to \uicontrol {All + Combined}.
            \li Search for \uicontrol {Interoperability}.
            \li Under \uicontrol {Swift Compiler - Language}, set
            \uicontrol {C++ and Objective-C Interoperability} mode to \uicontrol
            {C++/Objective-C++}.
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \section3 Add via Package.swift

    \list 1
        \li Specify a path to the cloned Qt Bridge repo in the dependencies
        section.
        \li Link \c QtBridge product to your target.
        \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability in \c swiftSettings with
        .\c {interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)} mode.
    \endlist

    \badcode
    dependencies: [
        .package(path: "path/to/qtbridge-swift")
    ],
    targets: [
        .target(
            name: "MyApp",
            dependencies: [
                .product(name: "QtBridge", package: "qtbridge-swift")
            ],
            swiftSettings: [
                .interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)
            ]
        )
    ]
    \endcode

    If you don't have access to the Qt internal repository, follow the tutorial
    below to build the local Qt package and specify it as a dependency for Qt
    Bridge.

    \section2 Building local Qt package

    \section3 1. Create Qt package folder

    Navigate to the cloned repository, copy Qt folder and place it next to the
    \c qtbridge-swift directory:

    \badcode
        cd /path/to/qtbridge-swift
        cp -R Qt ..
    \endcode

    This newly created Qt folder is our local Qt package and will be
    referred to as \c {/path/to/Qt} in these instructions.

    \section3 2. Download and build Qt 6.10.0 from source

    Check out the tutorial on how to
    \l {https://wiki.qt.io/Building_Qt_6_from_Git}{build Qt from source} or
    follow the simplified instructions below.

    Navigate to the directory that will contain the top-level \b qt6 repository
    and run the following command to clone it:

    \badcode
        git clone git://code.qt.io/qt/qt5.git qt6
    \endcode

    Qt Bridge depends on \b {Qt 6.10.0}, so switch to the corresponding branch:

    \badcode
        cd qt6
        git switch 6.10.0
    \endcode

    Next, fetch the submodule source code by running the following command from
    the \c qt6 directory:

    \badcode
        init-repository
    \endcode

    Create a separate build directory parallel to the source directory:

    \badcode
        cd ..
        mkdir qt6-build
        cd qt6-build
    \endcode

    From the build directory, configure, build, and install Qt 6. Pass the
    desired installation path using the \c -prefix parameter:

    \badcode
        ../qt6/configure \
        -release \
        -prefix your_qt_install_dir \
        -nomake tests \
        -nomake examples \
        -no-feature-sql \
        -no-feature-network \
        -no-feature-qml-network \
        -no-dbus \
        --module-subset=qtbase,qtdeclarative

        cmake --build . --parallel
        cmake --install .
    \endcode

    \section3 3. Create .xcframeworks for the Qt package

    Navigate to \c {your_qt_install_dir/lib} folder and create .xcframework from
    each .framework:

    \badcode
        cd your_qt_install_dir/lib

        for fw in *.framework; do
            name="${fw%.framework}" \
            xcodebuild -create-xcframework \
                -framework "$fw" \
                -output "${name}.xcframework"
        done
    \endcode

    Place the newly created .xcframeworks into the
    \c {path/to/Qt/lib/Frameworks} directory:

    \badcode
        mkdir /path/to/Qt/lib/Frameworks
        mv *.xcframework /path/to/Qt/lib/Frameworks
    \endcode

    \section3 4. Copy Qt headers into the Qt package

    Copy `QtQmlIntegration` folder from \c {your_qt_install_dir/include} into
    the \c {path/to/Qt/include} directory:

    \badcode
        cp -R ../include/QtQmlIntegration /path/to/Qt/include
    \endcode

    \section3 Copy plugins into the Qt package

    Copy \c qml and \c plugins folders from \c your_qt_install_dir into the root
    of the Qt package:

    \badcode
        cp -R ../qml /path/to/Qt
        cp -R ../plugins /path/to/Qt
    \endcode

    \section3 Qt package is ready

    At this point, the Qt package is ready and its structure should look like
    this:

    \badcode
        Qt
        ├── Package.swift
        ├── bundle.swift
        ├── include
        │ ├── QtQmlIntegration
        │ └── _spmQtQmlIntegration.cpp
        ├── lib
        │ ├── Frameworks
        │ │ ├── QtCore.xcframework
        │ │ ├── QtGui.xcframework
        │ │ ├── QtQml.xcframework
        │ │ └── ...
        │ ├── _spmQtCore.cpp
        │ └── ...
        ├── plugins
        │ ├── platforms
        │ └── ...
        └── qml
        ├── QtCore
        └──...
    \endcode

    \section3 Modify Package.swift of the Qt Bridge

    If you prefer building from Xcode, edit \c {qtbridge-swift/Package.swift}
    and set the \c useLocalQt variable to \c true:

    \badcode
        let useLocalQt: Bool = true
    \endcode

    If you prefer building from the terminal, you can set an environment
    variable to use the local Qt package before building:

    \badcode
        export QTBRIDGE_USE_LOCAL_QT_PACKAGE="true"
    \endcode

    At this point, the Qt Bridge and Qt packages are ready, and any projects
    referencing Qt Bridge can be built successfully.

    \section1 Running examples

    The \b Examples directory contains simple projects implemented with Qt
    Bridge. For instance, to build and run MinimalApp:

    \badcode
        cd qtbridge-swift/Examples/MinimalApp

        xcodebuild \
        -project MinimalApp.xcodeproj \
        -scheme MinimalApp \
        -destination 'platform=macOS' \
        -derivedDataPath build \
        build

        open build/Build/Products/Debug/MinimalApp.app
    \endcode

    \section1 Using Xcode templates

    The \b Templates directory contains:

    \list
        \li \b {a project template} for starting a new Swift + QML macOS
        application
        \li  \b {file templates} for adding Swift models and QML views
    \endlist

    \section2 Installing the templates into Xcode

    Copy the \c Templates folder into
    \c {~/Library/Developer/Xcode/Templates/}.

    \section2 Creating a new project from the Qt Bridge project template

    \list 1
        \li Open Xcode.
        \li Choose \uicontrol {Create New Project...}
        \li Scroll down to \uicontrol {Qt Bridge} section.
        \li Select the \uicontrol {Qt Bridge} project template and click
        \uicontrol {Next}.
        \li Fill in the template options:
        \list
            \li \uicontrol {Product Name} – the name of the application (also
            used for the target name and bundle name).
            \li \uicontrol {Organization Identifier} – a reverse-DNS identifier
            such as com.example.
            \li \uicontrol {Model Type Name} – the Swift type used as the
            backend model, for example \c {MyModel}.
            \li \uicontrol {QML File Name} – the base name of the initial QML
            file, without the .qml extension (for example main or myView).
            \li \uicontrol {Model Context Name} – the name under which the
            model is exposed to QML, usually in lowerCamelCase (for example
            myModel).
            \li Choose a location for the project and click \uicontrol {Create}.
        \endlist
        \li The generated project will contain:
        \list
            \li an application entry point with \c {@main}
            \li a Swift model type with the name you provided
            \li a QML file with the chosen base name, already included in the
            app’s bundle resources
            \li a macOS app target with Swift/C++ interoperability enabled
        \endlist
        \li The project template sets up the application structure, but you
        still need to add the Qt Bridge as a package dependency.
    \endlist

    \section2 Running the example application

    When you use a Team in Xcode and enable automatic signing, Xcode may enable
    the \b {Hardened Runtime} for the generated macOS app target. With Hardened
    Runtime enabled, macOS enforces \b {library validation}, which restricts
    the app to loading only system libraries and libraries signed with a
    compatible signature. Qt Bridge loads additional Qt frameworks at runtime,
    and this can cause the app to fail to launch when library validation is
    enabled.

    If you see a code signing error, open the app target’s \uicontrol {Signing &
    Capabilities} tab, expand \uicontrol {Hardened Runtime}, and manually check
    \uicontrol {Disable Library Validation}.
    You can also disable Hardened Runtime by clicking \uicontrol {Delete} on
    the right side of the tab.

    At this point, the example application is ready to run. You can do so by
    pressing the \uicontrol {Run} button. The project template configures the
    basic build settings and target configuration. For more advanced
    configuration, you can adjust \uicontrol {Build Settings} in the project
    navigator.

    \section1 Stay in touch

    You can reach us in the Qt Forum, specifically in the
    \l {https://forum.qt.io/category/78/qt-bridges}{Qt Bridges category}.
*/
